<div class="tale-introduction"><p class="eyebrow">VAMANA &amp; KING BALI / THE THREE STEPS</p><p>This original English retelling follows the supplied Gita Press <em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Skandha 8, chapters 15–23. Ritual detail, hymns, battle passages and extended discourse are condensed; Bali’s promise and Vamana’s revelation as Trivikrama remain central.</p></div>
<section class="story-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title"><p class="eyebrow">MEET THE CHARACTERS</p><h2 id="characters-title">Characters in this story</h2><dl><div><dt>Vamana · Trivikrama</dt><dd>Vishnu appearing first as a small brahmachari and then revealing a form that encompasses the worlds.</dd></div><div><dt>King Bali</dt><dd>Prahlada’s grandson, a powerful asura ruler known here for generosity and fidelity to his word.</dd></div><div><dt>Aditi</dt><dd>Mother of the devas, whose disciplined vow precedes Vishnu’s appearance as her son.</dd></div><div><dt>Kashyapa</dt><dd>Aditi’s husband, who teaches her the payo-vrata described in the Bhagavata.</dd></div><div><dt>Shukracharya</dt><dd>Bali’s preceptor, who recognizes Vamana and warns the king about the consequence of the gift.</dd></div><div><dt>Vindhyavali</dt><dd>Bali’s queen, present when the promised land is formally given.</dd></div><div><dt>Prahlada</dt><dd>Bali’s grandfather, who arrives after the cosmic steps and recognizes grace within the loss of power.</dd></div></dl></section>
<div class="tale-layout"><aside class="tale-contents"><p class="eyebrow">IN THIS STORY</p><nav aria-label="Story chapters"><a href="#victory"><span>01</span>A kingdom regained</a><a href="#vow"><span>02</span>Aditi’s vow</a><a href="#arrival"><span>03</span>The brahmachari arrives</a><a href="#request"><span>04</span>Only three steps</a><a href="#promise"><span>05</span>A promise tested</a><a href="#trivikrama"><span>06</span>The measure of the worlds</a><a href="#third"><span>07</span>The third step</a><a href="#sutala"><span>08</span>A gift beyond a kingdom</a><a href="#traditions">Traditions &amp; interpretation</a><a href="/sources.html#vamana-king-bali">Sources &amp; reading notes</a></nav><a class="tale-back" href="/stories.html">Return to the collection</a></aside><article class="tale-body">{% include "reader-tools.njk" %}
<section class="tale-chapter" id="victory"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>01</span><h2>A kingdom regained</h2></div><p>After earlier defeat, Bali is restored through the guidance and rites of the descendants of Bhrigu. Strengthened by the Vishvajit sacrifice, he advances upon heaven. Indra and the devas withdraw, and Bali assumes sovereignty over the three worlds.</p><p>His rule is sustained through further sacrifices. Power, generosity and ritual authority gather around him, but the dispossessed devas turn toward another kind of refuge.</p><p>Aditi grieves for her sons. Kashyapa returns to the hermitage and listens to her sorrow—not only for lost status, but for a household emptied of those she loves.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="vow"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>02</span><h2>Aditi’s vow</h2></div><p>Kashyapa teaches Aditi the payo-vrata, a disciplined observance directed to Vishnu. The text gives its rites in detail; at its centre are restraint, worship, hospitality and devotion.</p><p>Aditi completes the vow with a concentrated mind. Vishnu appears before her in his four-armed form and acknowledges her longing. He promises to enter her family as a son while the strength of Bali’s allies makes a direct victory impossible.</p><p>In time, the Lord appears through Aditi and Kashyapa. Before their eyes, the radiant divine form becomes Vamana, a small brahmachari prepared for sacred study.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="arrival"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>03</span><h2>The brahmachari arrives</h2></div><p>Vamana receives the signs of a young seeker: sacred thread, belt, deer skin, staff, water pot, rosary and parasol. Having completed the rites expected of a brahmachari, he hears of Bali’s great sacrifice and sets out for the yajna ground.</p><p>The assembly notices him at once. Small in stature yet luminous with brahma-tejas, he seems to fill the pavilion with presence. Bali rises, welcomes him and washes his feet with reverence.</p><p>The king offers cattle, gold, villages, horses, elephants and whatever else the guest might desire. Vamana does not ask for wealth on that scale.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="request"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>04</span><h2>Only three steps</h2></div><p>Vamana praises Bali’s lineage and asks for a little land—three steps measured by his own feet. The king is surprised. Someone standing before the ruler of three worlds, he says, should ask for enough to secure a livelihood.</p><p>Vamana answers that possessions cannot satisfy a mind that has not learned contentment. One who is not satisfied with three steps, he says, would not be satisfied even by an island or a world.</p><p>Bali smiles at what seems a modest request and agrees. A gift small enough to overlook is about to reveal the scale by which ownership itself is measured.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="promise"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>05</span><h2>A promise tested</h2></div><p>Shukracharya recognizes Vishnu beneath the brahmachari’s appearance. He warns Bali that the three steps will take everything and advises him to withdraw the promise.</p><p>Bali listens, but decides that he cannot knowingly turn a gift into a falsehood. Wealth, position and life all pass away, he says; the dishonour of deceiving a guest after giving one’s word is what he fears.</p><p>Even when his preceptor curses him to lose his prosperity, Bali does not retreat. Vindhyavali brings the golden vessel. The king pours the water that completes the gift, and the gathered beings praise the difficulty of what he has chosen.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="trivikrama"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>06</span><h2>The measure of the worlds</h2></div><p>The small form begins to expand. The Bhagavata imagines the elements, directions, celestial beings and living worlds within the body of Trivikrama. The guest who asked for a measured patch of ground now appears as the measure within which every realm exists.</p><p>With one step he encompasses the earth and the directions. With the second he crosses the celestial realm. Brahma honours the raised foot with water, which the narrative connects with the heavenly Ganga.</p><figure class="tale-inline story-landscape"><img src="/assets/images/trivikrama-cosmic-step.webp" alt="Four-armed Trivikrama spanning earth and heaven while Brahma offers water at the raised divine foot and King Bali watches below" loading="lazy"><figcaption>TRIVIKRAMA ENCOMPASSES THE WORLDS · AI-GENERATED DEVOTIONAL INTERPRETATION</figcaption></figure><p>Nothing remains outside the two strides. Vamana’s third step has nowhere left to fall.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="third"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>07</span><h2>The third step</h2></div><p>Bali’s followers move toward conflict, but the king restrains them. The force of time that once raised their cause now moves another way, he says; anger cannot recover what has changed.</p><p>Bound by Varuna’s cords and stripped of sovereignty, Bali is asked to complete the promise. His answer is neither an excuse nor a claim that the first two steps were unfair. He offers his own head as the place for the third.</p><p>Prahlada arrives and bows before the Lord. Vindhyavali and Brahma also speak for Bali, recognizing that he has surrendered all he possessed without abandoning his word.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="sutala"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>08</span><h2>A gift beyond a kingdom</h2></div><p>Vishnu declares that Bali has remained steady through loss, rebuke, binding and abandonment. The test has not exposed a broken promise; it has revealed a devotion no kingdom could contain.</p><p>Bali is granted Sutala, described as a splendid realm protected from affliction. The Lord promises to remain near him there, guarding him and his people. The Bhagavata also looks forward: Bali will become Indra in a future Manvantara.</p><p>Released from the cords, Bali bows and enters Sutala with his companions. Indra regains heaven, yet Bali’s story does not close as simple defeat. What he loses as possession returns as a relationship with the divine presence itself.</p></section>
<section class="tale-reflection" id="reflection"><p class="eyebrow">EDITORIAL REFLECTION / PAUSE &amp; REFLECT</p><h2>A promise larger<br><em>than possession.</em></h2><p>Bali’s promise becomes costly only after it is spoken. What helps a commitment remain truthful when keeping it changes how we see everything we thought was ours?</p></section>
<section class="tale-reflection" id="traditions"><p class="eyebrow">TRADITIONS &amp; INTERPRETATION</p><h2>The small form.<br><em>The immeasurable stride.</em></h2><p>This reader follows the Vamana account in the supplied Bhagavata Purana, Skandha 8. It begins with Bali’s conquest and Aditi’s vow so the request for land appears within the chapter sequence that frames it.</p><p>Vamana is described as a brahmachari with the objects of sacred study; Trivikrama is the expanded cosmic form through which the worlds are measured. The four-armed Trivikrama in the interior painting is a devotional artistic convention. The text’s cosmic-body description is far more expansive than any single illustration can reproduce.</p><p>Ritual prescriptions, speeches and combat are condensed. The reflection on promises and possession is editorial contemplation. Later festival traditions and regional tellings are not silently merged into this Bhagavata retelling.</p><a href="/sources.html#vamana-king-bali">Read the supplied-edition references</a></section>
